# Kleefsman et al. (2005) MARIN 3D dam break with box obstacle (SPHERIC Test 2) -- pressure sensor P1
# Original reference: K.M.T. Kleefsman, G. Fekken, A.E.P. Veldman, B. Iwanowski, B. Buchner, "A Volume-of-Fluid
#   based simulation method for wave impact problems", J. Comput. Phys. 206(1), 363-393 (2005).
#   DOI:10.1016/j.jcp.2004.12.007
# Secondary source (string kleefsman_exp_data_p1 copied verbatim; fetched twice, identical):
#   PySPH, pysph/examples/db_exp_data.py
#   https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pypr/pysph/main/pysph/examples/db_exp_data.py
#   https://github.com/pypr/pysph/blob/master/pysph/examples/db_exp_data.py?plain=1
# DIGITISED from plots (PySPH module docstring: "The data is extracted from plots"), coarse (~0.42 T spacing
#   away from impact). NOT the original MARIN time series (that is test_case_2_exp_data.xls inside the SPHERIC zip).
# Nondimensionalisation -- CAUTION, PySPH is internally inconsistent:
#   db_exp_data.get_kleefsman_data() comment says "# p/(rho*g*H) v/s t*sqrt(2*g/H)", BUT the example that uses
#   it (pysph/examples/dam_break_3d.py, https://github.com/pypr/pysph/blob/main/pysph/examples/dam_break_3d.py?plain=1)
#   scales the simulation with factor_x = np.sqrt(9.81/H), factor_y = 1/(ro*9.81*H) and labels the axis
#   "t sqrt(g/H)". The plotting usage is taken as authoritative here:
#   T = t*sqrt(g/H) [-], Pn = p/(rho*g*H) [-], H = 0.55 m (fluid column height), g = 9.81, rho = 1000.
#   Conversion (NOT applied): t[s] = T/sqrt(9.81/0.55) = T/4.2233 ; p[Pa] = Pn*1000*9.81*0.55 = Pn*5395.5
# PySPH sensor location docstring: "Pressure probe readings for a probes at (2.42, 0, 0.021), (2.42, 0, 0.101)"
#   (PySPH coordinate frame; in dam_break_3d.py x_probe = obstacle_center_x - obstacle_length*0.5,
#    p_z = [0.021, 0.101]) -- i.e. P1 on the upstream (water-facing) box face, see kleefsman_2005_geometry.txt.
